course: biology
lesson: 105
title: The polar zone: polar bears
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Stand at the underwater window of the polar zone and a polar bear glides past, paddling with front paws as big as dinner plates and steering with its back legs, because in the wild it swims between pieces of sea ice to hunt seals.
explain: The bear looks snow white, but each hair is clear and hollow and only looks white because it scatters light, like snow does, and underneath the fur the skin is black and the fat is thick.
explain: Polar bears need the sea ice: they wait on it for seals to come up to breathe, and as the climate warms the ice melts earlier and freezes later, so the bears must go longer without food.
explain: Adverts show polar bears sipping fizzy drinks and cartoons put them next to penguins, but real polar bears hardly drink at all and live in the Arctic, at the opposite end of the Earth from wild penguins.
fact: Polar bears are strong swimmers that paddle with huge front paws and steer with the back legs, and one bear was tracked swimming for over nine days without stopping.
fact: Polar bear hairs are clear and hollow and look white because they scatter light; the skin underneath is black, and in some warm zoos tiny algae growing inside the hairs have turned the fur green.
fact: Keepers give polar bears ice blocks with fish, meat or fruit frozen inside, so breaking the ice keeps them busy, a bit like hunting; a wild bear can smell a seal under the snow from a long way off, often said to be about a kilometre.
fact: Wild polar bears roam huge distances, so they need deep, cool water and lots of space; small concrete pits in older zoos made many bears stressed, so many zoos stopped keeping them and newer ones built big areas with grass, hills and deep pools.
fact: Arctic sea ice now melts earlier and freezes later as the climate warms, so polar bears have less time to hunt seals, though they can go months without eating while they wait.
fact: Polar bears are the largest meat-eaters on land and may see people as food, and the Canadian town of Churchill has a bear patrol that people call instead of going near a bear, and a «polar bear jail» where bears that come into town are kept until they can be flown away.
fact: Polar bear cubs are born in a snow den in winter, blind and weighing less than a kilogram, and stay with their mother about two years; she does not eat for months while feeding them milk.
fact: The soles of a polar bear's paws have fur and small bumps that grip the ice and keep the feet warm.
fact: Polar bears evolved from brown bears, scientists think a few hundred thousand years ago, and polar bears and grizzlies have met in Canada and had «pizzly» cubs.
fact: Polar bears clean themselves carefully after eating and roll in snow in summer, because clean fur keeps them warm and dirty fur lets cold and water reach the skin.
fact: Polar bears drink very little: seawater is too salty and fresh water is frozen most of the year, so they get most of their water from breaking down the fat of seals.
exercise: How does a polar bear swim? | It paddles with its big front paws and steers with its back legs | It kicks hard with its big back legs and tucks its front paws in like a frog | It wiggles its body like a seal | It floats and lets the wind push it
exercise: Why must polar bears be good swimmers in the wild? | They swim between pieces of ice to hunt seals | They catch fish in deep water all day long, like otters | They migrate across the ocean every winter | They hide from wolves in the sea
exercise: What colour is a polar bear's skin under its fur? | Black | Pure white | Pale pink | Yellow
exercise: Why does polar bear fur look white? | Clear hollow hairs scatter light, like snow | The hairs are full of white paint-like colour | The bear rolls in chalk to look white | Snow sticks to every hair
exercise: Why did some zoo polar bears turn green? | Tiny algae grew inside their hollow hairs | They ate far too many green vegetables at the zoo | Keepers dyed them for a show | They rolled on fresh green grass
exercise: Why do keepers freeze fish inside big ice blocks? | It keeps the bear busy, like hunting | It is the only way to keep fish fresh | Polar bears cannot eat fish that is not frozen | So the bear can use the cold block as a pillow at night
exercise: Why did many zoos stop keeping polar bears in small concrete pits? | Bears walk huge distances and small pens stressed them | Polar bears hate concrete because it is grey | The grey concrete made their pool water far too warm in summer | Visitors could not see them in pits
exercise: Why are polar bears losing their hunting time? | Sea ice melts earlier and freezes later as it warms | Seals have learned to hide in deep caves under the ice | There are too many polar bears now | Ships scare all the fish away
exercise: Why should nobody treat a polar bear like a cuddly friend? | It is the largest land meat-eater and may see people as food | It is friendly, but it is far too big and heavy to hug safely | It is only dangerous to seals, never people | It bites only if you take its ice
exercise: What happens in the «polar bear jail» of Churchill, Canada? | Bears that come into town are held until flown away | Bears that hurt people are kept for ever | Visitors are locked up for a night for feeding the bears | Cubs are raised until they can swim
exercise: About how much does a newborn polar bear cub weigh? | Less than a kilogram | About 10 kilograms, like a big dog | About 50 kilograms, like a child | As much as a grown man
exercise: Why are polar bear soles covered in fur and bumps? | They grip the ice and keep the feet warm | They help the bear swim much faster | They stop the bear making footprints | They are for digging deep holes in rock
exercise: Which animal did polar bears evolve from? | Brown bears | Giant pandas | Harbour seals | Arctic wolves
exercise: Why does a polar bear clean its fur so carefully after eating? | Clean fur keeps it warm; dirty fur lets cold reach the skin | It wants to smell nice for visitors | Seal fat makes its fur fall out in big clumps | It is getting ready to sleep all winter
exercise: Where do polar bears get most of their water? | From breaking down the fat of the seals they eat | From drinking seawater every day | From licking melting icebergs all day long | From eating lots of snow
test: In a fizzy drink advert polar bears sip cola on the ice. What is real? | Polar bears drink very little and get water from seal fat | Polar bears drink lots of fresh water from the lakes every day | Polar bears drink seawater like whales | Polar bears need sweet drinks for energy
test: A cartoon shows a polar bear chatting with penguins on the ice. What is wrong? | Wild polar bears live in the Arctic and wild penguins in the south | Polar bears are far too shy to go anywhere near penguins on the ice | Penguins are too fast for polar bears | Nothing, they live together in Antarctica
test: «polar bears are white all over, even the skin, right» What is true? | The skin is black, and the clear hairs only look white | Yes, white skin and white hair | No, the skin is pink like a pig's | No, the skin is green from algae
test: A mother polar bear has 2 cubs. How many paws do the three of them have? | 12 | 8 | 6 | 16
test: What would happen to polar bears if the sea ice melted much earlier every year? | They would have less time to hunt seals and go hungry longer | They would hunt seals much more easily in the open summer water | They would all swim south and move to Antarctica | Nothing, they mostly eat berries
test: «aww the cub's tiny, bet it's born big as a dog» What is right? | Newborn cubs weigh less than a kilogram and are blind | Yes, they are born about the size of a big dog, with open eyes | Yes, and they can swim from the first day | No, they hatch from eggs in the snow
test: «bloody hell, a polar bear in town, let's go take a selfie» What should people do? | Stay away and call the bear patrol | Go close, but keep very quiet | Throw it food so it stays calm | Stroke it gently from behind
test: A «pizzly» bear was seen in Canada. What is it? | A cub of a polar bear and a grizzly | A pizza-eating zoo bear | A polar bear with green algae in its fur | A new kind of panda
test: 3 polar bears and 2 brown bears in a documentary. How many tails? | 5 | 3 | 10 | 20
test: Why might a polar bear in summer roll in the snow? | To clean its fur | To cool down after eating a big heavy meal | To hide from seals | To make its fur grow longer
test: A polar bear walks on slippery ice without falling. How? | Fur and small bumps on its soles grip the ice | It has metal-like claws only | It never walks on ice, only snow | It slides on purpose and enjoys it
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the fur and skin of the polar bear? | The hairs are transparent and hollow, appearing white by scattering light, and the skin beneath is black | The hairs contain a white colouring, and the skin beneath them is likewise white | The hairs are white and the skin is pink | The hairs are black and appear white owing to frost
exam: Identify the principal consequence for the polar bear of the earlier melting and later freezing of Arctic sea ice. | A shorter period in which to hunt seals and longer intervals without food | An abundance of seals in open water, rendering hunting easier than before | A migration of the species to the Antarctic | No consequence, the species feeding chiefly upon vegetation
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the source of water for the polar bear. | It obtains the greater part of its water from breaking down seal fat | It drinks seawater in considerable quantities daily | It drinks fresh water from frozen lakes and rivers throughout the year | It requires sweetened liquids supplied by man
exam: State the ancestry of the polar bear as presently understood by scientists. | It evolved from the brown bear, probably a few hundred thousand years ago | It evolved from the giant panda in the recent past | It descends from the seal upon which it now preys, some millions of years ago | It has existed unchanged since the age of the dinosaurs
